{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And if his hand cannot reach, etc.</b> Meaning: even though his hand can reach more than two turtledoves and two young pigeons, as long as his hand has not attained enough for a lamb minus an issar or minus a pundeyon, behold this one brings an offering of the poor." "And in Torat Kohanim they expounded <b>his hand</b> — they do not tell him to borrow and they do not tell him to engage in his trade, etc. The reason is because a mitzvah is precious in its time, and as they said of blessed memory (Torat Kohanim here; Pesachim 68a) concerning the verse ״וְאִם לֹא תַשִּׂיג יָדוֹ דֵּי שֶׂה״ — he has a lamb but lacks his needs: from where do we know that he brings an offering of the poor? Scripture says <b>דֵּי שֶׂה</b> — meaning, this man had a lamb, but it is for his own needs, as you would say it was for his clothing or for his sustenance; behold it is as though he does not have it. And this is what Scripture was precise in saying ״דֵּי שֶׂה״ — meaning, in his hand to bring a lamb; and even though he has the lamb, he does not have the power to bring it because he needs it." "And I saw that our master Hillel explained ״his needs״ — meaning, the needs of the lamb that he lacks, for the law is that he must attend to it to bring it to the Temple courtyard. And this is far-fetched, for what exertion is there in this more than bringing turtledoves and young pigeons? And if it is because of the size of the lamb — he can carry coins in his hand to the Temple and purchase it there. And if it is because the lamb requires semikhah (leaning of hands) — but semikhah is not me'akev (a barrier to validity), so why should he nullify his offering because of semikhah and bring an offering of the poor? And we learned at the end of tractate Negaim (Negaim 14:12): a rich man who brought an offering of the poor has not fulfilled his obligation. And although this was taught in tractate Negaim, the same law applies to every korban oleh veyored (sliding-scale offering), and so it is proven from the words of the Rambam in chapter 10 of Hilkhot Shegagot. But semikhah is merely shiyarei mitzvah (remnants of a mitzvah), and if he did not perform semikhah he has achieved atonement, as they said at the beginning of Zevachim (Zevachim 6a)." "<b>Further,</b> I saw that they expounded in Torat Kohanim in the parashah of the woman who gives birth ״אִם לֹא תִמְצָא יָדָהּ״ etc. — she has a lamb but lacks her needs: from where do we know that she brings an offering of the poor? Scripture says ״דֵּי שֶׂה״ — end of quote. Behold, this exposition is needed for the matter of nesachim (libations), for nesachim are me'akevin (a barrier), and the offering of the woman who gave birth is an olah that requires nesachim. From this point on, what is this inclusion that Scripture multiplies in this parashah of the chattat (sin-offering)? If it is for semikhah, it is difficult: why was it necessary to say ״דֵּי שֶׂה״ concerning the olah — and is it not a kal vachomer (argument from minor to major)? If in the case of the chattat, whose requirements are not me'akevin, all the more so should he bring an offering of the poor on its account — then for the olah, whose requirements are me'akevin, all the more so should he bring an offering of the poor! Rather, it certainly appears as I have written, and so I saw that the Rav, author of Korban Aharon, agreed."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {2 "I rendered שְׁיָרֵי מִצְוָה literally as 'remnants of a mitzvah' per the translator convention of not decoding rabbinic terminology."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:53:28.291420924Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}